-2\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043085/state.json" "file_sha256" "d53c0281f12463ce2db5b2adef4fe688a42354c25096c2e46e3e49ddeb4fcd5d" "basis" 1594 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043085/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-69979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the $4$th column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $4$th column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(G) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 6 & 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =(-2)\\cdot (1\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1))=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(G)=4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043085/state.json" "file_sha256" "d53c0281f12463ce2db5b2adef4fe688a42354c25096c2e46e3e49ddeb4fcd5d" "basis" 1594 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043085/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-69979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043085/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9502466162106389e038b09703e666fcc9e8197d9f9412d7a83a86